WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:22.966
You know, a lot of times, when you--as a scientist--think back to experiments that you've done

00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:30.999
even really successful experiments, you realize that at the time that you were doing them,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.699
you didn't really fully understand the science involved or the science behind them

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.199
or the science which was going to affect whether you were going to get results or not,

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.799
and you probably also didn't really appreciate how lucky you were.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.732
Today, we're going to talk a little bit about one experiment--a wonderful experiment--

00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:59.132
that I did many years ago with Christianne Nüsslein-Volhard

00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:02.832
at a time when we were both starting out our scientific careers.

00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:06.532
We, like many people at the time (this was the late 1970s)

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:10.599
were interested in the problem of embryonic development,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:11.866
but at a very basic level.

00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:15.999
We knew that genes controlled things that you could see happening in the embryos.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.599
We wanted to identify what those genes were and what they did.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.566
And there were a lot of different molecular strategies--conceptual strategies--

00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:25.599
that you could use to do this.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.932
We decided that we would try a genetic approach and what that meant for us

00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:38.799
was that to build on our lack of knowledge and to just randomly mutagenize all genes--

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.432
knock them all out and see what happens,

00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:51.599
and get some estimate at least or some global picture of what it was the genes were doing during development,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.632
how gene activities were organized in patterns and in sequences

00:01:56.633 --> 00:02:01.999
to produce these extraordinary phenomena that you see in development.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.532
We knew that there were lots of genes,

00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:09.766
and we knew that understanding the process would probably require that we identify most of them,

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:17.532
so we had set up in our minds at least this goal that we wanted to identify everything.

00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:21.632
We wanted to identify all the genes, and this meant a really big experiment.

00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:25.999
And for those of you who have any sense or experience working with Drosophila,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.799
what a big experiment looking for lots of genes means is lots of tubes,

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.666
growing lots of flies, and setting up crosses.

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:44.266
Basically, our plan was to feed flies mutagens, establish inbred lines from single males

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:48.399
in the first generation and then carry it through an appropriate number of generations

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:56.199
to produce inbred heterozygous lines that we could collect homozygous embryos from.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:01.132
And we thought about it, and our estimates of what we needed to do was something like

00:03:01.133 --> 00:03:07.466
in the range of maybe 40,000 lines established through many generations.

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:10.432
And for us to do that, we realized

00:03:10.433 --> 00:03:16.199
(and this is actually the first real secret and the unappreciated aspect of this experiment

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.466
is that we were starting our jobs in Heidelberg)

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:26.099
and we knew that we had to set up lots of tubes, and we would identify all these genes

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:34.766
and if it was going to be effective, we would have to have ways of almost a mechanized inbreeding of flies.

00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:37.832
We couldn't sit and set up crosses manually by hand.

00:03:37.833 --> 00:03:40.632
We would have to have selective procedures that allowed us

00:03:40.633 --> 00:03:48.199
to avoid collecting individual flies and set up crosses.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:54.732
The first two years of our stay in Heidelberg was involved in this.

00:03:54.733 --> 00:04:02.232
Setting up and designing genetic crosses that allowed us simply to put a fly in a tube.

00:04:02.233 --> 00:04:06.932
Wait two weeks, there would be lots of flies... Put two flies in a tube, actually.

00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:11.666
Wait a couple of weeks for the next generation,

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:15.999
and by raising it at a different temperature, or with some other selective technique,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.666
we could just shake the surviving flies over and go through and gradually inbreed the lines

00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:25.099
such that, at the end of the cross down here at the bottom, after three generations,

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.399
we had balanced, inbred lines so that we could collect homozygous embryos.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:35.232
Now, it took us two years to set that up and to get crossing schemes that would actually work.

00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:38.499
We set out to do the experiment, and the experiment actually, itself only took us...

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:43.966
well, two rounds of experiments, each of which were less than two months in advance.

00:04:43.967 --> 00:04:49.399
So, I guess the first lesson is the idea that if you have a vision and the vision's clear enough

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:59.132
you may still have to spend a lot of work just getting the assays and getting the procedures right.

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:01.832
But then the next thing, and this is the thing that I really wanted to talk about

00:05:01.833 --> 00:05:10.599
We did actually start out with trying to make 40,000 lines.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:15.966
As you'll see, we only really carried about 27,000 of them through to the third generation

00:05:15.967 --> 00:05:21.599
But, by the time we got to that generation and began to get our first results...

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:22.032
We'd gotten our first important result.

00:05:22.033 --> 00:05:29.832
We'd used enough mutagenesis that, in the lines we were looking at,

00:05:29.833 --> 00:05:35.966
we had about, in these 27,000 lines, we had about 18,000 different mutations.

00:05:35.967 --> 00:05:41.599
So that meant that our mutagenesis procedures worked--everything was working fine

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.666
Our interest, though...these mutations kill the fly at any time during its life cycle,

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:52.299
and what we were really interested in were genes that controlled the way embryos developed.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:56.932
And, this is the point, actually, where I think we were extraordinarily lucky.

00:05:56.933 --> 00:06:02.699
Because, what we had to do was now, from all these 18,000 different lines

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:08.332
we had to collect mutant embryos, look at the embryos that die,

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:11.099
and decide whether the gene that was mutated in those flies

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.366
actually killed the embryos and killed them in an interesting way.

00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:18.032
And the great result--the thing we couldn't have anticipated

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:24.866
was that, of the 18,000 different lethal mutations, only a tiny, tiny fraction

00:06:24.867 --> 00:06:28.999
actually changed homozygous embryos in a way that was really meaningful.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:35.866
Most of the time, the homozygous mutant embryos hatched and crawled away.

00:06:35.867 --> 00:06:41.999
It didn't have to be that way, and what would have been really, really horrible for us, in thinking back to it

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.499
is if every one of those 18,000 lines, we picked up and looked at embryos and there was some phenotype...

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:49.232
something had gone wrong.

00:06:49.233 --> 00:06:54.666
We would never have actually been able to, I think, since there was really only the two of us doing these experiments

00:06:54.667 --> 00:06:58.032
to sort things out and come up with a coherent picture.

00:06:58.033 --> 00:07:04.499
So, we were really lucky that what had actually happened was that, in a way that we didn't fully anticipate,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.499
the screen was very selective...

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:12.132
selective for mutations that caused phenotypes in homozygous embryos.

00:07:12.133 --> 00:07:18.432
That is to say, we were selecting for genes that had to, themselves, be transcriptionally active in embryos,

00:07:18.433 --> 00:07:23.299
and that, in flies, that number is very small.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:27.432
And so, the number of mutations was about 586,

00:07:27.433 --> 00:07:34.132
and actually the number of genes, if you do complementation tests among those mutations,

00:07:34.133 --> 00:07:38.066
there are only about... we only found 139 different genes.

00:07:38.067 --> 00:07:42.432
So that's a really small number that reduced the problem

00:07:42.433 --> 00:07:48.232
of thinking about development down to 139 components--139 genes,

00:07:48.233 --> 00:07:50.699
each of which were producing different phenotypes

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:56.032
and could be arranged in pathways and sequences that made, for me at least,

00:07:56.033 --> 00:08:02.499
the whole process of development almost understandable or structural for the first time.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:07.799
That understanding came from the fact that the numbers are small.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:11.499
And the numbers are small, not because we planned them that way.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:17.099
The numbers are small because of the peculiar biological features... the way flies develop.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:22.032
It turns out that flies' embryonic development is very rapid, the mothers puts everything the embryo needs...

00:08:22.033 --> 00:08:26.899
[everything] that she can possibly put into the egg is put in by the mother,

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:33.432
and the transcriptional requirements early during these patterning stages are very minimal.

00:08:33.433 --> 00:08:40.932
So, the fly has stripped down its transcriptional requirements to the rare genes that can't be supplied

00:08:40.933 --> 00:08:46.866
by the mother--that must be supplied by transcription in this cell and not in this cell.

00:08:46.867 --> 00:08:53.632
There's genes...the embryo is using transcription to control patterns, to control transitions in time.

00:08:53.633 --> 00:09:01.099
So, we did a genetic experiment that was oriented towards identifying phenotypes in homozygous embryos

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:04.999
and because of the biology of flies--the way they work--

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:12.599
we ended up identifying the central set of core regulatory genes

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:16.832
whose activity temporally and spatially control the development of pattern.

00:09:16.833 --> 00:09:22.366
And if I think back to what both Janni [Nüsslein-Volhard] and I, at the time...

00:09:22.367 --> 00:09:25.332
We thought a lot about this experiment.

00:09:25.333 --> 00:09:31.099
As I said, we planned it for more than two years, we actually spent two years trying to get the thing set up,

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:36.732
and yet we didn't really fully understand how the system worked--

00:09:36.733 --> 00:09:40.499
how the fly embryos developed until we actually had the numbers

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:41.299
and had the results.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:44.166
And I think that's actually a great lesson in biology...

00:09:44.167 --> 00:09:50.632
On the one hand, you don't understand the experiment really that you're doing

00:09:50.633 --> 00:09:51.966
until you get the result.

00:09:51.967 --> 00:09:58.232
And it's really only when you get the result that you potentially understand

00:09:58.233 --> 00:10:02.832
what biology and the whole system that you work with is actually giving you.

00:10:02.833 --> 00:10:13.232
That's true, and I guess it's pretty much the nature of science.

00:10:13.233 --> 01:00:00.000
It's really great to do experiments where you don't know the outcome.

